    "result_text": "The only executable test in the project is `AcceptanceTest` (the other files under `acceptance/` are support classes). The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field on owners so it returns `true` when any of the three risk signals holds, and `false` otherwise.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `riskFlag` read-only boolean property to the `Owner` schema (which drives the generated `OwnerDto`), documenting the three trigger conditions.\n\n2. **`EmailDomainClassifier.java`** \u2014 Added `isDisposableAdjacent(email)`, which flags a domain that shares its *registrable label* (the label just before the final TLD) with a known disposable domain \u2014 i.e. the same provider under a different TLD (`mailinator.net`) or a subdomain of one (`inbox.mailinator.com`). These slip past the exact-match `isDisposable` check that rejects creates, so they're exactly the near-neighbours worth flagging at read time.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Injected `EmailDomainClassifier` and added:\n   - `riskFlag(owner)` \u2014 OR of the three signals, wired via `@Mapping(target = \"riskFlag\", ...)`.\n   - `cityOverSoftCapacity(owner)` \u2014 counts owners in the same city (using the same `IdentityUtils.normalizeIdentity` key the create-time per-city rule uses) and returns true when it exceeds the soft capacity of 40 (the hard per-city cap enforced on create is 50).\n\n**Interpretation notes** (where the spec left room):\n- *Possible duplicate* maps directly to the stored `possibleDuplicate` flag.\n- *Disposable-adjacent* is deliberately distinct from *disposable*: exact disposable domains are already rejected with 400 on create, so a stored owner can only ever carry an adjacent (not exact) domain \u2014 I defined it as sharing the registrable label.\n- *Soft capacity* = 40, sitting below the existing hard cap of 50; consistent with the existing `capacityWarning` (40\u201349) semantics. It's computed live in the mapper, matching how other derived fields (locality, membershipLevel) are computed.\n\nSince it's a derived read-only field, the normal single-owner case (`example.test` email, unique city) yields `false`, satisfying `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally`.",
